<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RELEASE-ONLY MANIFEST OVERLAY. Gradle merges this over src/main/AndroidManifest.xml for the
  `release` build type only; debug builds are untouched.

  🚨 **A RELEASE BUILD OF AN OFFLINE SIGNER MUST NOT HOLD THE INTERNET PERMISSION.**
  `android.permission.INTERNET` is granted silently at install — no prompt, no user choice — and
  it is the single switch that decides whether this process can open a socket at all. The CSP in
  tauri.conf.json already refuses outbound requests, but that is the APP enforcing a rule on
  ITSELF: code running inside the webview (a compromised bundled dependency, say) is the thing
  deciding whether to honour it. Removing the permission moves the same rule down to the kernel,
  where nothing running in this app gets a vote. Defence in depth, and the outer layer is the one
  an attacker cannot reach.

  ⚠️ **DEBUG BUILDS KEEP IT, DELIBERATELY.** `tauri dev` loads the UI from a dev server over
  http://localhost:1420 (via `adb reverse`), which genuinely needs the permission. A release
  build serves the UI from inside the APK through Tauri's own asset handler, so no socket is
  involved. That asymmetry is exactly why this file is scoped to `release` rather than the line
  being deleted from the main manifest.

  ⚠️ **THIS IS THE ONE CHANGE IN THE 2026-09-18 ADVERSARIAL PASS THAT A BUILD CAN DISPROVE.**
  Everything else there was verified by tests; this rests on "a release build needs no socket",
  which only a real release APK can confirm. If that is ever wrong the app opens to a BLANK
  SCREEN, and it fails only in release, so nothing in `tauri dev` would show it. Smoke-test a
  release APK on a device or emulator after touching this: launch it, and open the QR scanner
  once (CAMERA is a separate permission and should be unaffected, but it is the one feature that
  touches hardware). A broken release is fixable by publishing the next version, but sideloaded
  installs do not auto-update, so whoever downloaded it stays broken until they notice.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />
</manifest>
